FADE IN:

# TEASER

INT. THE OLSON ESTATE — THE FRONT FOYER — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 3:07 AM EST

Darkness. The grandfather clock ticks. Its hands say seven minutes past three.

The front door opens as quietly as a front door on West Paces Ferry can open. Mallory slips in, heels dangling from one hand, a Velvet staff hoodie over her jumpsuit. She eases the door shut. She turns.

The lamp on the hall table clicks on.

Peter sits on the bottom step of the staircase, exactly where he was at eleven forty-four. He's still in the cashmere sweater. He looks like he hasn't moved.

MALLORY
(frozen)
Daddy.

PETER
(quietly)
It's three o'clock.

MALLORY
I know.

PETER
You said midnight.

MALLORY
I know. I texted Grace to—

PETER
Grace called me at twelve-fifteen. She was crying. She said you told her to cover for you, and she couldn't lie to me, because I was the one who taught her to ride a bike.

Mallory closes her eyes.

MALLORY
Daddy, I'm sorry. Nothing happened. I swear to God. We sat on a roof and talked.

PETER
For three hours.

MALLORY
(beat)
He talks a lot.

Peter almost smiles. It doesn't take.

PETER
Who is he?

MALLORY
The DJ.

PETER
What's his name?

MALLORY
(beat)
Q.

PETER
Q what?

MALLORY
He wouldn't say.

Peter stands up slowly. He walks to her. He takes the heels out of her hand and sets them on the hall table.

PETER
Mallory. Listen to me.
(beat)
A man who won't tell you his name has a reason.

MALLORY
Maybe his reason is his family. Maybe he's tired of being somebody's son. You don't know what that's like.

PETER
(beat, very quietly)
Don't I?

She looks at her father. His eyes are red. It isn't anger.

MALLORY
(softly)
You were scared.

PETER
I'm a surgeon. I've spent twenty years sewing up what happens to people at three o'clock in the morning.
(beat)
I was terrified.

She puts her arms around him. He holds on, hard.

PETER (CONT'D)
(into her hair)
Next time, you call me. I'll come get you. I don't care where you are. I don't care who he is.

MALLORY
Okay.

PETER
Promise.

MALLORY
I promise.

He lets her go. He kisses her forehead.

PETER
Go to bed. Your mother lands at ten-forty tomorrow, and if she finds out about this, we'll both need a surgeon.

Mallory laughs, wet. She picks up her shoes and runs up the stairs.

Peter stands alone in the foyer. He turns off the lamp.

In the dark, his cell phone lights up on the hall table. One missed call. +52 998.

He looks at it. He doesn't touch it.

CUT TO:

INT. THE DOWAGER HOUSE — THE MORNING PARLOR — MORNING

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 7:34 AM EST

The pale-yellow parlor. On the round table, beside the silver coffee service, a small round cake on a crystal stand. White buttercream. Piped in blue icing: "7."

Simone sits at the table in lavender, beaming. Joan sits beside her in a gray suit and the everyday pearls. The Washington pearls are gone.

The door opens. Victor walks in, in a navy suit, freshly shaved. He stops. He looks at the cake.

VICTOR
(flat)
Mother.

SIMONE
(delighted)
Seven! A new record!

VICTOR
You had a cake made.

SIMONE
Cleo made it. I supervised. Sit down and eat your cake, Victor, you've earned it.

He sits. Cleo appears, cuts a slice, and sets it in front of him with great ceremony. He looks at Joan.

VICTOR
Did you know about this?

JOAN
(sipping her coffee)
I suggested the number.

Victor picks up his fork. He eats a bite of cake at seven-thirty in the morning with enormous dignity.

VICTOR
(chewing)
It's very good, Cleo.

CLEO
Thank you, sir. Welcome home, sir.

She goes. Simone refills her own cup.

SIMONE
Tonight you sleep in your bed.

VICTOR
So I'm told.

SIMONE
And you'll stop calling mayors.

VICTOR
(beat)
I'll stop calling mayors about my granddaughter.

JOAN
Victor.

VICTOR
That's the best I can do, Joan. I'm sixty-eight years old. The mayor is a phone call away. She's always been a phone call away.

Joan and Simone exchange a look. Simone lets it pass.

SIMONE
(lightly)
The Smilley girl wrote to me.

Victor's fork stops.

VICTOR
Esther Smilley?

SIMONE
A thank-you note. For my invitation. Delivered by hand, yesterday morning, before eight. Her man wouldn't wait for an answer.

VICTOR
What did she say?

SIMONE
(beat, sipping)
That it was the opposite.

VICTOR
The opposite of what?

SIMONE
(smiling into her cup)
That's the part she didn't say.

Victor looks at his mother. Ninety-four years old, pleased with herself, a step ahead of every person at the table.

VICTOR
Mother. What are you doing?

SIMONE
Eating cake. With my son. On the morning he comes home.
(beat)
Eat yours, Victor. You're going to need your strength. Your granddaughter's having a very big day.

CUT TO:

INT. CITY HALL EAST — APD HEADQUARTERS — CHIEF'S CONFERENCE ROOM — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 9:00 AM EST

A windowless conference room. A long table, a whiteboard, a coffee urn, and a flag in the corner. The blinds on the glass door are shut.

Elxa sits at the head of the table in full uniform. Jeremy sits at her right with a legal pad.

Around the table, the four DEPUTY CHIEFS: TOLBERT (50s, heavy, rumpled), KEMP (40s, bald, precise), WASHINGTON (50s, a woman with reading glasses on a chain), and ALBRIGHT (40s, a white man with a mustache). And at the foot of the table, directly opposite Elxa, Hughes.

In front of each of them, a manila folder: CONFIDENTIAL — COMMAND STAFF — BLACKWATER TASK FORCE — EYES ONLY. Each has a name typed on the tab.

ELXA
Good morning. Thank you for being here. I know the Council kept you late yesterday.
(beat, a glance at Hughes)
Some of you longer than others.

A few small smiles around the table. Hughes doesn't smile.

ELXA (CONT'D)
Everything the Blackwater Task Force has as of this morning is in front of you. Nine pages. Read it here. It doesn't leave this room. The folders stay on the table when you go.

TOLBERT
(opening his)
We can't take notes?

ELXA
You can take notes, Deputy Chief. In your head.

They open their folders. The only sound is pages turning.

Elxa watches them read. She doesn't look at Hughes. She doesn't have to. Jeremy does it for her, without lifting his eyes from his legal pad.

Page one. Page two. Page five.

Page six.

Tolbert reads his page six. His eyebrows go up: "a second, prepaid cell phone."

Kemp reads his: "a partial print." He nods once, satisfied.

Washington reads hers: "a red sedan." She makes a small mark on the page with her fingernail.

Albright reads his: "the widow hired a PI." He frowns.

Hughes reads page six.

INSERT — HUGHES'S PAGE SIX: "The Chief believes the coin recovered at Piedmont Park is a FAKE..."

He reads it twice. A small muscle moves in his jaw. That's all.

He turns to page seven.

ELXA
(evenly)
Questions?

KEMP
The partial print. Is it in AFIS?

ELXA
Running now.

WASHINGTON
The red sedan. Do we have a plate?

ELXA
Partial. We're working it.

HUGHES
(pleasantly, closing his folder)
Chief. Page six.

The room quiets.

HUGHES (CONT'D)
Your "assessment." I'd like to understand it. You don't believe the Blackwater Syndicate killed these men?

Elxa holds his eyes down the length of the table.

ELXA
I believe what the evidence tells me, Deputy Chief. When it tells me something new, I'll believe that instead.

HUGHES
And if the press asks?

ELXA
(beat)
The press won't ask. Because nobody at this table is going to tell them.

A long beat. Hughes smiles.

HUGHES
Of course not.

ELXA
(standing)
Thank you. Folders stay on the table.

The deputy chiefs stand, leave their folders, and file out. Hughes is last. He stops at the door.

HUGHES
Good meeting, Chief.

ELXA
Thank you, Warren.

He goes. The door closes.

Elxa looks at the clock on the wall. 9:41 AM.

JEREMY
(quietly)
How long?

ELXA
(sitting down, picking up her coffee)
If it's him?
(beat)
Less than an hour.

SMASH CUT TO:

> MAIN TITLES — "KINGDOM" <

===

# ACT ONE

INT. CITY HALL EAST — APD HEADQUARTERS — DEPUTY CHIEF HUGHES'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 9:52 AM EST

A real office, with windows: plaques, a signed photo of Hughes with former Chief Harold Jackson, a framed 1988 Atlanta Journal front page ("COP SAVES BABY FROM HOLLYWOOD RD. BLAZE"), and a golf putter in the corner.

Hughes closes the door behind him. He locks it.

He stands at the window for a moment, looking down at Ponce de Leon Avenue.

Then he takes a flip phone out of his inside jacket pocket. Not the department BlackBerry on his belt. His own.

He dials a number from memory.

HUGHES
(into phone, low)
It's me.
(beat)
You didn't get this from me.
(beat)
The Chief doesn't think Blackwater did it. She thinks the coin's a fake. A copycat. She told the command staff this morning. In writing.
(listening)
No. Page six. "Chief's assessment."
(beat)
Because the city deserves to know their new Chief thinks the most dangerous gang in the South is being framed.
(beat)
Run it at noon.

He hangs up. He stands there, holding the phone. For a moment he looks like a man who's just done something he'll regret.

Then he looks at the 1988 front page on his wall. The young cop with the baby in his arms.

HUGHES (CONT'D)
(quietly, to the photograph)
Twenty-five years.

He puts the phone back in his pocket.

CUT TO:

INT. CITY HALL EAST — APD HEADQUARTERS — CHIEF'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 10:04 AM EST

Elxa sits behind her desk with her arms folded. Jeremy stands beside her. Both watch the small TV on the filing cabinet. WBKG.

The morning show's cooking segment, a chef flipping crêpes, cuts abruptly to a red graphic: "BREAKING NEWS."

WBKG ANCHOR (ON TV)
We interrupt this program with a Channel 8 exclusive. Sources inside the Atlanta Police Department tell Channel 8 that Chief Elxa Jackson privately believes the black onyx coins found with two murder victims this month are FAKES.

Jeremy exhales.

WBKG ANCHOR (ON TV) (CONT'D)
According to a confidential command-staff document, the Chief believes the killings may be the work of a third party posing as the so-called Blackwater Syndicate. APD has not responded to our request for comment.

The graphic: "SOURCES: CHIEF BELIEVES 'BLACKWATER' COIN IS A FAKE."

Elxa watches it without moving.

ELXA
(quietly)
Page six.

JEREMY
Twenty-three minutes after he walked out of the room.

ELXA
He didn't even wait for noon.

JEREMY
(beat)
Somebody at WBKG couldn't.

Elxa picks up the remote. She turns off the TV.

She sits in silence for a long moment.

JEREMY (CONT'D)
(carefully)
Elx. Do you want me to get his badge?

ELXA
(beat)
No.

JEREMY
No?

ELXA
Not today.

Jeremy stares at her.

JEREMY
He just leaked a confidential document on live television.

ELXA
He leaked a true document on live television. The only true one.
(beat)
If I fire him today, I lose the only microphone I've got that I know is plugged in. Tomorrow, somebody else leaks, and I don't know who.
(beat)
If I keep him, I decide what goes into the microphone.

Jeremy looks at his wife for a long moment. Slowly, he starts to smile.

JEREMY
You're going to feed him.

ELXA
I'm going to feed him.

JEREMY
(beat)
My father would say that's cold.

ELXA
Your father would say that's a closed door and a pension. I'm keeping both of my promises.

Her desk phone RINGS. She glances at the display. FBI — ATLANTA. She picks up.

ELXA (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Jackson.

DOYLE (V.O.)
(filtered, dry)
I read it in the paper. Well, I watched it on TV. Between the crêpes.

ELXA
I told you you would.

DOYLE (V.O.)
(beat)
So you caught your mole.

ELXA
I caught my mole.

DOYLE (V.O.)
And?

ELXA
And he's staying right where he is.

A pause on the line.

DOYLE (V.O.)
(slowly)
Chief. That's either the dumbest thing I've ever heard a cop say or the smartest.

ELXA
(beat)
Call me in a month and I'll tell you which.

She hangs up. Jeremy is grinning outright now.

JEREMY
She likes you.

ELXA
(picking up a file)
Nobody likes me, Jeremy. They respect me, and it'll do.

CUT TO:

INT. DONOHUE TOWER — MARTIN'S OFFICE — 86TH FLOOR — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 10:19 AM EST

Black glass. The white orchid. On the bank of four TVs, WBKG loops the red graphic: "SOURCES: CHIEF BELIEVES 'BLACKWATER' COIN IS A FAKE."

Martin stands in front of the screen with his hands in his pockets. He's perfectly still. For once, he isn't pleased.

He crosses to his desk. He unlocks the drawer. He takes out the black phone with no display.

He dials a long number. Not international this time. Local.

He waits. Then:

MARTIN
(into phone, very calm)
Turn on Channel Eight.
(beat)
Yes. That.
(listening)
She knows it's a fake. She knew last week. And now the whole city knows she knows.
(listening)
No. It doesn't change anything. It changes the story.
(beat)
Two dead men who don't matter to anybody north of I-20. A deacon and a loan shark. The papers were bored by Monday.
(beat)
The city believes what it's afraid of. And right now it isn't afraid enough.
(listening, then, with quiet precision)
Then we give them something they can't call fake.
(beat)
I'll bring the details tomorrow night. Same place. Quarter to twelve.
(beat)
No. Not a deacon this time.

He hangs up. He puts the phone back in the drawer and locks it.

He turns back to the TV. The graphic is still there. Under it, a still photo: Elxa at her swearing-in, hand raised, four stars on her collar.

MARTIN (CONT'D)
(softly, to the screen)
Clever girl.
(beat)
Too clever for your own good, cousin.

He turns off the TV.

CUT TO:

INT. KEEL & ASSOCIATES REALTY — BUCKHEAD — HARMONY'S CUBICLE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 11:08 AM EST

Harmony at her cubicle by the restroom, in a new charcoal suit, on the phone with a Smilley acquisitions binder open in front of her.

HARMONY
(into phone)
—no, the city owns the three south lots outright. I need the probate docket on the Reese estate. The heirs in Detroit—yes. Thank you. Call me back.

She hangs up. A PROCESS SERVER (50s), in a Braves windbreaker, is standing at her cubicle wall holding a thick envelope.

PROCESS SERVER
Harmony Divine?

HARMONY
(warily)
Who's asking?

PROCESS SERVER
(handing it over)
You've been served, ma'am. Have a nice day.

He's gone before she can say anything. Misty, three cubicles down, watches over the partition with naked delight.

Harmony looks at the envelope. FULTON COUNTY SUPERIOR COURT — FAMILY DIVISION.

She tears it open. Her eyes race down the first page.

INSERT — THE ORDER: "IN RE: LYRIC SOLACE BAKER, A MINOR CHILD. PETITION FOR GRANDPARENT VISITATION, GLORIA A. BAKER, PETITIONER... ORDER SETTING HEARING: MONDAY, MARCH 14, 2005, 9:00 A.M.... FURTHER ORDERED that a family evaluation be conducted by court-appointed evaluator DR. MIRIAM KESSLER, PH.D., who shall interview the child's parents, the petitioner, and the child..."

Harmony reads it once. Then again. Her hand is shaking.

She grabs her cell phone. She dials. It rings once.

AMOND (V.O.)
(filtered, surprised)
Harmony?

HARMONY
(low, furious)
You did this.

INTERCUT WITH:

^INT. BSI TOWER — AMOND'S OFFICE — 58TH FLOOR — CONTINUOUS

A big office down the hall from Marc's: modern, spare, a view of Midtown. A framed photo of Lyric on the pony on the credenza. Amond stands at his desk in shirtsleeves.

AMOND
Did what?

HARMONY
I just got served. At my desk. In front of the whole office. A hearing. March fourteenth. And a family evaluation. A court psychologist is going to come to my house and watch me feed my daughter.

AMOND
(stunned)
Harmony, I didn't—

HARMONY
You sent flowers Tuesday night. Peonies. "Proud of you."
(beat)
And Thursday morning I get served.

AMOND
I didn't know. I swear to God, Harmony, I didn't know anything about this.

HARMONY
Your mother's name is on it.

AMOND
My mother told her lawyer not to file anything. Tuesday night. I know she did. My father told me.

HARMONY
Then why am I holding it?

A beat. Amond has no answer.

HARMONY (CONT'D)
(her voice breaking)
One Sunday a month. That's what she offered. Like my daughter's a timeshare.
(beat)
Tell your mother the answer is no. Not "not yet." No.

AMOND
Harmony—

HARMONY
And don't send me any more flowers.

She hangs up.

In his office, Amond stands holding the dead phone. He looks at the photo of Lyric on the pony.

He grabs his coat.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT TWO

INT. THE BAKER HOME — 214 MAGNOLIA TERRACE — KITCHEN — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 12:21 PM EST

The Baker kitchen at lunchtime. Gloria, in a cardigan, is arranging African violets on the windowsill. Curtis sits at the table in scrubs, eating a sandwich between shifts.

The back door BANGS open. Amond comes in with the court papers in his hand, still in his coat.

GLORIA
(delighted)
Baby! You never come home for lunch—

AMOND
(holding up the papers)
Mama. What is this?

Gloria sees the court seal. Her face falls.

GLORIA
Where did you get that?

AMOND
Harmony's lawyer faxed it to mine an hour ago. Harmony got served at her desk. In front of her whole office. A hearing. March fourteenth. And a court psychologist.
(beat)
She called me. She thinks I did it.

GLORIA
(taking the papers, reading, horrified)
I told Stanley not to file anything. Tuesday night. I called him from this kitchen. Your father was sitting right there.

CURTIS
(quietly, setting down his sandwich)
Glo.

GLORIA
I did, Curtis. You heard me.

CURTIS
I heard you. Tuesday.
(beat)
Last Thursday you told him to request a hearing. The day Harmony said "not yet." You were so angry you called him from the car.

Gloria stops.

CURTIS (CONT'D)
He filed the request last Thursday. The court set the date this week. That's all this is. The court doing what you asked it to do a week ago.

GLORIA
(sinking into a chair)
Oh, Lord.

AMOND
(beat)
So you did do it.

GLORIA
I did it last week. I undid it Tuesday. I didn't know you can't undo a court.

CURTIS
You can. You withdraw the petition.

Gloria looks up at her husband. Then at her son.

GLORIA
(beat)
No.

AMOND
Mama.

GLORIA
No, Amond. I'm not withdrawing it. I'm sorry she found out at her desk. I'm sorry about that. I'll write her a letter and say so.
(beat)
But I'm not giving up the only way I have of seeing my granddaughter. She's had two years to let me in. She let me in eleven times.

AMOND
She offered—

GLORIA
She offered nothing. I offered one Sunday a month, and she said "not yet." And this morning, I hear, she said no.

AMOND
(beat)
She said no.

GLORIA
Then a judge can decide.

A heavy silence. Curtis looks at his wife for a long moment.

CURTIS
(quietly)
Glo. When Amond was two, my mother wanted him every Sunday. Remember what you said to her?

Gloria doesn't answer.

CURTIS (CONT'D)
You said, "Mrs. Baker, with respect, you'll see him when I say you'll see him, and not one day before, and if you don't like it, you can take it up with Jesus."

GLORIA
(stung)
That was different.

CURTIS
It was exactly the same. You were twenty-seven and scared, and my mother had an opinion about everything you did.
(beat)
Harmony's twenty-two.

Gloria stares at him. Her eyes fill.

GLORIA
(barely)
I just want to hold her.

AMOND
(softening, crouching beside her chair)
I know, Mama.

GLORIA
(touching his face)
You look just like her. Did you know that? She has your face.

AMOND
(beat)
I know.

Gloria looks at the court papers in her lap.

GLORIA
I'll write the letter. The apology.
(beat)
But the petition stays.

Amond stands. He looks at his father. Curtis shakes his head slightly. Not today.

AMOND
(quietly)
Okay, Mama.

He goes out the back door. Curtis picks up his sandwich and doesn't eat it.

CUT TO:

INT. CHILDREN'S HEALTHCARE OF ATLANTA AT EGLESTON — PEDIATRIC ONCOLOGY PLAYROOM — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 1:12 PM EST

The playroom. Paper snowflakes. The fish tank. Marc on the alphabet rug in shirtsleeves with a second hand-bound book: THE DINOSAUR PRINCESS AND THE DRAGON. The cover: the same purple grape in a tiara, now next to a green blob with wings.

Keyonna, in pink pajamas, leans against his knee. DeAndre, with his IV pole, sits a skeptical foot away. Nurse Tonya leans in the doorway.

MARC
Okay. Chapter two. Before we start, the Princess needs a name for the dragon. Keyonna.

KEYONNA
(ready, very serious)
Gorgeous.

Marc's mouth opens. Closes.

MARC
(beat)
Gorgeous?

KEYONNA
You said it on the phone. In the hallway. Before Christmas. "Hey, Gorgeous." You sounded happy.
(beat)
The dragon should sound happy.

Nurse Tonya covers her mouth.

MARC
(recovering, laughing)
That was my great-grandmother. She's ninety-four.

DEANDRE
You call your great-grandma Gorgeous?

MARC
Every Monday.

DEANDRE
(grudging)
That's kind of cool.

MARC
(to Keyonna, solemnly, writing on the title page with a marker)
The dragon's name is Gorgeous. And she's ninety-four years old. And she's the smartest dragon in the Kingdom of Snacks.

KEYONNA
(beaming)
Yes.

Marc opens the book. His phone buzzes in his pocket. AMOND. He lets it go to voicemail.

MARC
(reading, big voice)
"One morning, the Dinosaur Princess woke up and found a dragon sitting on her roof, eating all the chimneys—"

It buzzes again. AMOND. Then a text.

INSERT — THE TEXT: "911. not blackwater. harmony."

Marc closes the book gently.

MARC (CONT'D)
(to the kids)
Five minutes. Nobody tell Gorgeous anything without me.

INT. EGLESTON — THE HALLWAY OUTSIDE THE PLAYROOM — CONTINUOUS

Marc walks and calls, phone to his ear.

MARC
(into phone)
Talk.

AMOND (V.O.)
(filtered, in his car)
She got served. At her desk. Mama's petition. A hearing in March and a court psychologist. Harmony called me and said, "You did this." Then she told me not to send any more flowers.

MARC
Did you do it?

AMOND (V.O.)
No! Mama did it last week when Harmony said "not yet." Then she changed her mind Tuesday. But you can't change a court's mind.
(beat)
And now Mama won't withdraw it. She says a judge can decide.

Marc stops at the window at the end of the hall. Outside, a little boy in a wheelchair is being pushed around the courtyard by his father.

MARC
(beat)
Mond. Your mother's not the villain.

AMOND (V.O.)
I know she's not the villain. She's my MOTHER.

MARC
She's a grandmother who's held her granddaughter eleven times. Everybody in this story is right, and that's what makes it hard.

AMOND (V.O.)
(beat)
So what do I do?

MARC
You go to Harmony's door tonight. Not with flowers. Not with lawyers. With something of Lyric's.
(beat)
And you tell her the truth. You didn't know. Your mother made a mistake. And you're not going to fight her in court. Whatever your mother does.

AMOND (V.O.)
(beat)
I'm not?

MARC
Are you?

AMOND (V.O.)
(long beat)
No.

MARC
Then tell her.

AMOND (V.O.)
(beat)
When did you get good at this?

MARC
(watching the boy in the courtyard)
I read a lot of children's books.

He hangs up. He stands at the window a moment.

Then he goes back to the playroom, to a dragon named Gorgeous.

CUT TO:

INT. THE OLSON ESTATE — THE CATERING KITCHEN — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 1:53 PM EST

The stainless-steel kitchen. A double boiler bubbling with batch two of the rose balm. Amber glass sample jars lined up on the counter with handwritten labels.

Mallory, in safety goggles and an oversized sweater, eyes shadowed from three hours of sleep, stirs. Grace sits on the counter in sweats and sunglasses, holding a coffee, glaring.

GRACE
You. Left. Me.

MALLORY
I told you to go.

GRACE
You TEXTED me to go. From a ROOF. With a MAN.

MALLORY
He's a DJ.

GRACE
Named Q!

MALLORY
So?

GRACE
(taking off her sunglasses)
Mallory Simone Olson. "Q" is not a name. "Q" is a serial-killer name. "Q" is what they call the guy on Law and Order who keeps body parts in a freezer.

MALLORY
(laughing despite herself)
He doesn't keep body parts in a freezer.

GRACE
How do you KNOW? You don't know his LAST NAME.

MALLORY
I know he's from Chicago. The part with the lake.

GRACE
(beat)
That's all of Chicago.

MALLORY
That's what I said!

Grace hops off the counter and comes around to the double boiler. She looks at her cousin's face. Really looks.

GRACE
(slowly)
Oh no.

MALLORY
What?

GRACE
You're glowing.

MALLORY
I'm tired.

GRACE
You're tired AND glowing. That's the worst combination. That's how Mama looked in the pictures from her honeymoon.

MALLORY
(stirring, pink)
Nothing happened, Grace.

GRACE
Did he kiss you?

MALLORY
No.

GRACE
Did he TRY?

MALLORY
(beat)
No.
(beat)
He asked if he could walk me to the cab. And then he asked if he could call me. And then he wrote his number on my hand because I wouldn't give him mine.

She holds up her left hand. On the palm, faded Sharpie: a 312 number and a single letter. Q.

GRACE
(staring)
He wrote it on your HAND.

MALLORY
I didn't wash it.

GRACE
(beat)
Oh, you're in trouble.
(beat, softer)
Did Uncle Peter kill you?

MALLORY
(the smile fading)
No. He was waiting on the stairs. He didn't yell. He just...
(beat)
He looked scared, Gracie. Daddy never looks scared. He sews people's faces back together.
(beat)
And he's been getting phone calls from Mexico. He doesn't answer them.

GRACE
Mexico?

MALLORY
A 998 number. I looked it up. Cancún.

Grace takes that in.

GRACE
Maybe it's a patient.

MALLORY
(stirring slowly)
That's what he said.

A beat. The rose balm bubbles. Grace sniffs it.

GRACE
(gently changing the subject)
It's better than batch one.

MALLORY
(looking up)
It is?

GRACE
It smells like the Dowager House now. Like Sunday.
(beat)
Call him.

MALLORY
Who?

GRACE
The serial killer. Call him. Just don't go on any more roofs without telling me first. I'm your cousin. I'm legally obligated to be on the next roof.

Mallory laughs. She looks at the number on her palm.

CUT TO:

INT. FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE — DISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 2:00 PM EST

The DA's office. Arianna sits at her conference table, not behind her desk. Jacket on. Hands folded. Composed. She looks like a DA about to depose someone.

Carla sits across from her with a yellow legal pad, a pen, and her thermos. On the corkboard over Carla's shoulder, Dee grins down at both of them.

CARLA
Thank you for doing this, Madam District Attorney. I'm asking everybody on the Henderson list the same four questions. Word for word. You're first.

ARIANNA
That's the right way.

CARLA
(pen ready)
Question one. Were you a guest at the home of Dr. and Mrs. Walter Henderson, 3244 Northside Drive, on the night of December 31, 2001?

ARIANNA
Yes.

CARLA
(writing)
Question two. At approximately what time did you leave, and by what route did you drive?

ARIANNA
(evenly)
Around two. A little after. I took Northside south to West Paces Ferry and turned west, toward home.

CARLA
(writing)
South on Northside. Toward the curve.

ARIANNA
(beat)
Toward West Paces. Yes.

CARLA
You were driving.

ARIANNA
I was driving.

CARLA
The designated driver.

ARIANNA
(a small smile)
I told Bernadette Sinclair a lot of things at midnight.

Carla smiles too. She writes.

CARLA
Question three.
(beat, looking up, her voice exactly the same)
Between approximately one-thirty and three o'clock that morning, did you see anyone walking on Northside Drive?

The office is very quiet. The radiator ticks.

Arianna holds Carla's eyes.

ARIANNA
(beat)
No.

CARLA
(still looking at her)
No one.

ARIANNA
It was dark. There are no sidewalks on that stretch. There are no streetlights past the Hendersons' gate. I was watching the road.
(beat)
I didn't see anyone.

Carla holds her look a moment longer. Then she writes on the pad.

INSERT — THE LEGAL PAD, Carla's neat hand: "Q3 — 'No.' (2.5 sec.) Volunteered: dark, no sidewalks, no lights, watching road."

BACK TO SCENE

CARLA
Question four. Is there anyone you believe I should speak with?

ARIANNA
(beat)
The valets. They'd have seen every car that left and which way it turned. And the off-duty officers on the door.

CARLA
(writing)
That's what I'd say too.
(capping her pen)
That's it. Four questions. Thank you, Madam District Attorney.

ARIANNA
(standing)
That's it?

CARLA
(standing, gathering her thermos)
That's it. The same four for all two hundred and eleven.
(beat)
Most people take about ninety seconds. You took four minutes. But you're a lawyer. Lawyers answer the question and then the question after the question.

Arianna's smile doesn't change.

CARLA (CONT'D)
(at the door)
One more thing. Not a question. Just making conversation.

ARIANNA
Of course.

CARLA
(pleasantly)
That was a pretty silver dress. In Bernadette's picture.
(beat)
What were you driving back then? A girl your age, from your family. Something nice, I bet.

ARIANNA
(beat)
A Volvo. My father thought it was safe.

CARLA
(nodding)
Fathers do.
(beat)
Thank you again.

She goes. The door closes.

Arianna stands at the conference table. She doesn't sit down. She looks at the door.

Then, slowly, she turns and looks at Dee's photograph on the corkboard.

ARIANNA
(barely a whisper)
I'm sorry.

She says it to the photograph. Nobody else hears.

CUT TO:

INT. CARLA'S BUICK — PARKED — FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE GARAGE — CONTINUOUS

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 2:09 PM EST

The maroon Buick LeSabre in a concrete parking deck. Carla sits behind the wheel with the legal pad on her knee and a cell phone to her ear.

CARLA
(into phone)
Darlene. It's Carla Mims. I'm with the DA's office now.
(listening)
I know. I know. Retirement didn't take.
(beat)
Darlene, I need a title history. A Volvo. Registered in Fulton County to a Cummings. C-U-M-M-I-N-G-S. First name Arianna, or the father, Franklin. Probably a 2000 or 2001 model.
(listening)
I want to know what happened to it. Sold, traded, totaled. Whatever the state knows.
(beat)
How long?

GEORGIA DMV RECORDS CLERK (V.O.)
(filtered)
The old records are on the mainframe in the basement, hon. Ten days. Two weeks if Frank's out.

CARLA
Two weeks is fine.
(beat)
And Darlene. Keep it between us. It's probably nothing.

She hangs up. She looks at the legal pad on her knee. "Q3 — 'No.' (2.5 sec.)"

She sits in the dim garage for a while.

CARLA (CONT'D)
(to herself)
It's probably nothing.

She starts the car.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT THREE

INT. SMILLEY TOWER — 71ST FLOOR — ESTHER'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 3:06 PM EST

The white office. Across Peachtree, Donohue Tower. Harmony paces in front of the curved glass wall holding the court order. Esther sits on the edge of her white desk, reading a copy.

HARMONY
—at my DESK, Esther. Misty Calloway watched the whole thing over the cubicle wall like it was the Super Bowl. And a psychologist. A court psychologist is going to sit in my living room and take notes on whether I cut Lyric's grapes right.

ESTHER
(reading)
Dr. Miriam Kessler.

HARMONY
You know her?

ESTHER
I know the type.
(setting the order down)
Harm. Stop pacing. You're wearing a hole in the carpet I haven't finished paying for.

Harmony stops. She drops into one of the white leather chairs.

HARMONY
I called him. I told him he did it.

ESTHER
Did he?

HARMONY
(beat)
He said he didn't know. He sounded like he didn't know.

ESTHER
(beat)
Then he probably didn't.

HARMONY
It's his mother's name on the paper.

ESTHER
Yes. And it's his mother's petition, and his mother's lawyer, and his mother's grandbaby.
(beat)
Harmony. Listen to me. For three years you've been fighting the Bakers in parking decks. With your hands. With your mouth. With me on the phone telling you to make them earn it.

HARMONY
You did say that.

ESTHER
I did. And I was right, and now I'm telling you something else.
(beat)
Let a judge say it.

HARMONY
(beat)
What?

ESTHER
Stop fighting them yourself. Let a judge look at you. A mother with a job, a clean house, a daughter who's happy and fed and loved and has a pony. And let a judge look at them. A grandmother who filed a petition on a toddler whose mother is looking for her missing brother.
(beat)
And then let the judge say it. Out loud. In a courtroom. Where it counts.

HARMONY
(slowly)
And if the judge says Gloria gets Sundays?

ESTHER
(beat)
Then Lyric gets a grandmother on Sundays.
(beat)
There are worse things, Harm. I had one.

Harmony looks at her. Esther looks back, calm.

HARMONY
(beat)
When did you get so wise?

ESTHER
I'm a mother of two.
(beat)
And I have a very good lawyer. You'll have him by five o'clock.

HARMONY
Esther—

ESTHER
Don't argue. Mr. Kincaid charges eight hundred dollars an hour, and he owes my father a favor from 1991. He's already read the file.

Harmony laughs, tearing up. She stands and hugs Esther hard.

HARMONY
(into her shoulder)
I don't know what I'd do without you.

ESTHER
(holding her, eyes closed)
You'll never have to find out.

Harmony pulls back, wiping her eyes. She picks up her things.

HARMONY
The Reese probate. I'll have it tomorrow.

ESTHER
Good. Go.

Harmony goes. The door closes.

Esther's face changes the instant it does. The warmth stays, but something harder comes up behind it.

The inner door to her private conference room opens. Crowe comes out. He's been waiting. He carries a thin manila envelope.

CROWE
Last night. The Ritz. Your uncle.

He lays four glossy photographs on the white desk, one after another. Drugstore prints from a disposable camera. Grainy but clear.

Desmond at the corner table with a cognac.

A young Black man in a charcoal overcoat sitting down across from him.

The two of them talking.

And later, near midnight, the two of them sitting by the fire, leaning toward each other.

ESTHER
(very quietly)
Who is he?

CROWE
Martin Donohue Olson. Twenty-three. Senior vice president at Donohue Global Holdings. Congresswoman Olson's son. The eldest grandchild of Victor Donohue.
(beat)
And, since 1996, the second-best grandson.

Esther picks up the last photograph. Martin's face in the firelight. Not smiling.

ESTHER
The one who didn't get the crown.

CROWE
Yes, ma'am.

ESTHER
(beat)
What were they talking about?

CROWE
My man couldn't hear. They talked for eleven minutes at nine. Then they sat on opposite ends of the room for nearly three hours.
(beat)
Then your uncle walked over and sat down again. They talked for eight minutes. Your uncle did most of the talking. Mr. Olson listened.
(beat)
My man says that at the end, Mr. Olson stopped smiling.

Esther sets the photograph down. She stares at it.

ESTHER
My uncle told Mr. Olson something.

CROWE
Yes, ma'am.

ESTHER
Something about me.

CROWE
(beat)
I don't know that, ma'am.

ESTHER
(evenly)
I do.

Silence. Across Peachtree, the Donohue crown catches the afternoon sun.

CROWE
What would you like me to do?

ESTHER
(beat)
Nothing.

CROWE
Ma'am?

ESTHER
If I confront my uncle, he'll know I'm watching. If I tell my father, it'll kill him.
(beat)
Keep a man in that bar every night my uncle's in it. And find out everything about Martin Olson that isn't on the DGH website.

CROWE
(beat)
And the CEO of BSI?

ESTHER
(sharply)
We've discussed that.

CROWE
Ma'am, if Mr. Olson is interested in you, and you've been to his cousin's gate twice—

ESTHER
(standing)
Thank you, Thaddeus.

He stops. He picks up the envelope, leaving the photographs.

CROWE
Yes, ma'am.

He goes. Esther stands alone with four photographs of her uncle and a stranger.

She picks up the one of Martin by the fire. She holds it up to the window, next to the Donohue crown across the street.

ESTHER
(softly)
What did he tell you?

CUT TO:

INT. SMILLEY TOWER — 69TH FLOOR — DESMOND'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 3:54 PM EST

The minimalist office. The Basquiat. Desmond sits behind his desk reading the Wall Street Journal, tranquil.

The door opens without a knock. Quentin wanders in: sunglasses, a Velvet hoodie over a T-shirt, a coffee, sneakers that cost more than most cars' tires. He's been awake for about forty minutes.

QUENTIN
Dad. Your assistant said you wanted me. I thought somebody died.

DESMOND
(not looking up)
Sit down, Quentin.

QUENTIN
(dropping into a chair, throwing a leg over the arm)
Is this about the board meeting? I told Aunt Vivienne, I don't do mornings. My two votes are yours. Use them however you want.

DESMOND
(folding the paper)
It's not about the board meeting.
(beat)
Where were you last night?

QUENTIN
Working.

DESMOND
Until three?

QUENTIN
The club closes at three, Dad. That's what "working" means when you're a DJ.

DESMOND
Your set ended at twelve-thirty. The manager told my assistant.

QUENTIN
(beat)
Then I was working on something else.

DESMOND
(studying him)
You're smiling.

QUENTIN
I'm always smiling.

DESMOND
You're never smiling before noon.

QUENTIN
It's four o'clock.

DESMOND
(beat)
That's what I mean.

Quentin laughs. He takes off his sunglasses. His eyes are tired and happy.

QUENTIN
Dad. It's nothing. I met somebody. We talked. That's it.

DESMOND
Who?

QUENTIN
(beat)
Nobody you'd know.

DESMOND
I know everybody.

QUENTIN
(standing, putting the sunglasses back on)
Then you'll find out on your own. You always do.

He heads for the door.

DESMOND
Quentin.

Quentin stops.

DESMOND (CONT'D)
(evenly)
This city isn't Chicago. In Chicago, we were the only family that mattered. Here, there are two. And every girl in Buckhead is related to one of them.
(beat)
Be careful who you talk to until three in the morning.

Quentin looks at his father.

QUENTIN
(beat)
You too, Dad.

DESMOND
(beat)
What does that mean?

QUENTIN
(a small shrug)
Somebody at Velvet said they saw your car at the Ritz last night. Till midnight.
(beat)
You never stay anywhere till midnight.

He goes. The door closes.

Desmond sits very still. Then he picks up the Journal again.

He doesn't read it.

CUT TO:

INT. WBKG CHANNEL 8 — NEWSROOM — CELESTE'S DESK — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 4:22 PM EST

A busy local newsroom: rows of desks, a wall of monitors, producers shouting. On every screen, the red banner from this morning is still running: "SOURCES: CHIEF BELIEVES 'BLACKWATER' COIN IS A FAKE."

Celeste sits at her desk, which is plastered with Post-its, in a camel turtleneck, with her phone to her ear. She's looking at the banner with visible irritation.

CELESTE
(into phone, low)
It wasn't my story. It came in on the desk line. Hank took it. He ran it at ten-oh-four without calling the Chief's office.

INTERCUT WITH:

^INT. DONOHUE TOWER — MARTIN'S OFFICE — 86TH FLOOR — CONTINUOUS

Martin sits back in his leather chair, one ankle on his knee, looking out at Smilley Tower.

MARTIN
(pleasantly)
I didn't say it was your story. I said it was a good one.

CELESTE
It's a leak. Leaks aren't stories. They're weather.

MARTIN
(smiling)
That's very good. You should put that on a mug.
(beat)
You didn't return my call yesterday.

CELESTE
I was at lunch.

MARTIN
With my cousin. South City Kitchen. He had the shrimp and grits. You stole a fried green tomato.

Celeste goes still.

CELESTE
(beat)
Are you having me followed, Martin?

MARTIN
I'm having lunch reservations followed. It's a small city, Celeste. Everybody in Midtown saw you.
(beat)
How's your count going?

CELESTE
(guarded)
It's going.

MARTIN
Three of seven Harlow defendants. Eleven of nineteen gambling cases. Four of four fire suits. Bullock and Associates.
(beat)
You told me you needed one more number.

CELESTE
I'm working on it.

MARTIN
Work on it in Savannah.

CELESTE
(beat)
Savannah?

MARTIN
The Port of Savannah files a public record for every container that clears customs. The importer. The broker. And the customs attorney of record.
(beat)
Look at who the attorney of record is on the Donohue Shipping containers that came in from Durban last fall.

Celeste grabs a pen. She writes on a Post-it: "SAV. PORT — DURBAN — ATTY OF RECORD."

CELESTE
(slowly)
Why would you tell me that? That's your own family's shipping line.

MARTIN
(beat)
Because I believe in a free press.

CELESTE
(dry)
No, you don't.

MARTIN
(laughing softly)
No. I don't.
(beat)
But I believe in you, Celeste. And I believe my family is big enough to survive the truth.
(beat)
Some of it, anyway.

CELESTE
(beat)
And Marc?

MARTIN
(pleasantly)
Marc runs a security company. What would Marc have to do with a law firm's customs filings?

A pause. Celeste looks at the Post-it.

CELESTE
I'm having dinner with him tomorrow night.

MARTIN
Then have a lovely dinner. And don't talk about work. My cousin hates it when people talk about work.

He hangs up. He sits back and looks out at the Smilley crown.

In the newsroom, Celeste stares at the dead phone. Then she peels the Post-it off the desk and folds it into her wallet, behind her driver's license.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT FOUR

INT. BELLE RIVE — NATASHA'S STUDY — DAY

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 4:41 PM EST

The peacock-blue study. Afternoon light. Natasha sits at the Louis XV writing desk in a camel suit, the phone at her ear, a legal pad in front of her.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
(filtered)
Mrs. Bullock. I asked a friend in parking.

NATASHA
Go on.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
Ms. Rhodes drives a black 2003 Jaguar XJ8. Georgia plates. It's registered to the firm.
(beat)
Every Thursday night since March of 2000, that car has entered the Colony Square parking garage in Midtown between seven-thirty and eight-thirty in the evening. It leaves between five-thirty and six the next morning.

Natasha writes: "COLONY SQ. — THURS. — 8 PM to 6 AM."

NATASHA
Every Thursday.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
Almost every. She missed Thanksgiving. And three weeks in the summer of 2002.

NATASHA
(beat)
Robert and I went to Martha's Vineyard for three weeks in the summer of 2002.

A silence on the line.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
(carefully)
Yes, ma'am.

NATASHA
(evenly)
And his car?

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
Mr. Bullock doesn't park in the garage, ma'am. Or if he does, it's not under his own name or his plates. I checked.

NATASHA
He's careful.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
He's a defense attorney, ma'am.

NATASHA
(beat)
What's within walking distance of the Colony Square garage?

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
Three hotels. The Four Seasons on Fourteenth. The Colony Square Hotel, which is attached to the garage. And the Peachtree Midtown Plaza across the street.
(beat)
I don't have anybody inside any of them yet.

NATASHA
Yet.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
Yet, ma'am.

Natasha looks at the legal pad. She looks at the calendar on her desk. Thursday, January 13.

NATASHA
(quietly)
It's Thursday.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
(beat)
Yes, ma'am. Would you like me to put someone on the garage tonight?

A long pause. Natasha stands. She goes to the window and looks down at the empty motor court.

NATASHA
No.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
Ma'am?

NATASHA
I'm not going to chase my husband through a parking garage on a Thursday night like a woman in a Lifetime movie, Nathaniel.
(beat)
When I go to Midtown, I'll know exactly which door to knock on. And I'll own the door.

NATHANIEL GREER (V.O.)
(beat)
Yes, Mrs. Bullock.

NATASHA
Find me the hotel. Take all the time you need. Just not too much.

She hangs up. She stands at the window.

In the leather datebook on her desk, under Thursday: "R. — late."

CUT TO:

INT. BSI TOWER — 57TH FLOOR — "THE GHOST ROOM" — DUSK

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 5:36 PM EST

Blue light. Server hum. Keisha in her chair. Marc leans over her shoulder. Roman stands behind them, arms folded, still in his coat.

On one monitor, WBKG's banner: "CHIEF BELIEVES 'BLACKWATER' COIN IS A FAKE."

On another: Hughes's personal cell phone records, in a column.

KEISHA
Nine fifty-two AM. Deputy Chief Warren Hughes's personal cell. Four minutes, eleven seconds. To the WBKG assignment desk.
(beat)
The meeting ended at nine forty-one. He made it eleven minutes.

MARC
(quietly)
She got him.

KEISHA
She got him clean. Five folders, five page sixes. I pulled the building's key-card log. Everybody else went back to their offices and made normal calls. Hughes locked his door.

MARC
(almost to himself, proud)
She set a canary trap on her first real week on the job and didn't tell a soul but her husband and her DA.

ROMAN
And she didn't fire him.

MARC
(a slow smile)
No. She didn't.

KEISHA
Why not?

MARC
Because now she's got a microphone she controls. Every word she lets Hughes hear goes straight to WBKG. She can put anything she wants on the six o'clock news.
(beat)
That's not a mistake. That's a weapon.

ROMAN
(dry)
She's your sister.

MARC
She's better than me. She doesn't have to lie to anybody to do it.

A beat. Keisha glances up at him.

MARC (CONT'D)
Roman. The Waffle House.

ROMAN
(stepping forward, handing Keisha a VHS tape)
The manager's a deacon at Friendship Baptist. He's been robbed four times. He likes Blackwater. He gave me Monday's tape for two hundred dollars and a promise nobody would hurt his cook.

Keisha slides the VHS into a deck. The monitor flickers. Grainy black-and-white security footage from a camera mounted over a Waffle House register. Timestamp: 01/10/05 12:14.

A booth by the window. Teddy Merriweather in a camel overcoat, facing the camera.

Across from him, back to the camera: a tall man in a dark canvas jacket and a baseball cap pulled low. Broad shoulders. Short hair at the nape. He sits perfectly straight, both hands flat on the table.

He never turns his head. Not once in the minute of footage Keisha fast-forwards through.

KEISHA
(frustrated)
He knew where the camera was.

ROMAN
He picked the seat.

MARC
(leaning closer)
Look at his hands.

On the screen, the man's hands rest flat on the tabletop the whole time. Utterly still.

ROMAN
(quietly)
Military. Hands where everybody can see them, so nobody gets nervous. Back to the wall's what you want, but back to the camera's what you take when the wall's got a window.

KEISHA
Can you tell me anything?

ROMAN
Right-handed. Six-two, six-three. Hundred and ninety. Doesn't eat. Look, he ordered coffee and didn't touch it.
(beat)
And he paid in cash and left a tip. Exact change. Twenty percent.

MARC
(beat)
Professional.

ROMAN
Professional. And polite.
(beat)
The polite ones are the worst.

Marc straightens up. He looks at the frozen image: the back of a head, a cap, two hands flat on a Formica table.

MARC
Ghost. Johannesburg.

KEISHA
Tonight. One AM our time. Their office manager turns on the order computer at eight AM Joburg.

MARC
(beat)
Then by tomorrow, I want the name of whoever paid for those coins.
(beat)
And Roman. Pack a bag.

ROMAN
Where?

MARC
Savannah.

CUT TO:

INT. RAVENHURST — LUCIUS'S STUDY — DUSK

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 6:04 PM EST

The dark study. The fire. Portraits of Cornelius and young Odessa.

Lucius sits at his desk in his shirtsleeves. In front of him, the same stack of documents from Monday.

Vivienne stands at the fire with a glass of wine, watching.

Lucius picks up the heavy gold pen with his right hand. He holds it a moment. His hand is steady.

He signs.

A clean, strong, elegant signature. Lucius C. Smilley. The same one that's been on Smilley International's annual reports since 1987.

He stares at it. He signs the next page. And the next. Every one perfect.

LUCIUS
(quietly)
Vivienne.

VIVIENNE
(setting down her glass)
I see it.

LUCIUS
(a disbelieving laugh)
Look at that. Look at that.

He holds up his right hand. Steady as a surgeon's. He turns it over, back and forth, like a man who's found a lost ring.

LUCIUS (CONT'D)
Harrington said I'd have good days.

VIVIENNE
(crossing to him)
He did.

LUCIUS
He said I'd have good days that would make me think he was wrong.

VIVIENNE
(beat)
He did.

LUCIUS
(looking up at her, eyes shining)
He's wrong.

VIVIENNE
(gently)
Darling.

LUCIUS
Let me have it. Just tonight. Let me have one night where he's wrong.

Vivienne looks at her husband. She takes his steady right hand in both of hers. She kisses the knuckles.

VIVIENNE
(softly)
Tonight he's wrong.

Lucius laughs. He pulls her down onto his knee like a man half his age. She laughs too, surprised.

For a moment they're just a husband and wife in a firelit room, laughing.

Then Vivienne turns her face into his shoulder so he can't see it.

Her eyes are full.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT FIVE

INT. CITY HALL EAST — APD HEADQUARTERS — CHIEF'S OFFICE — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 6:43 PM EST

Elxa sits behind her desk. The WBKG banner has finally been replaced on the TV by the weather.

A knock. Hughes stands in the doorway, cap under his arm. His face is carefully neutral.

HUGHES
You wanted to see me, Chief.

ELXA
Come in, Warren. Close the door. Sit down.

He hesitates at "sit down." He's never been asked before. He closes the door and sits.

ELXA (CONT'D)
You saw the news this morning.

HUGHES
(evenly)
I did. Terrible. Somebody at that table has a big mouth.

ELXA
(beat)
Somebody does.

A long silence. Hughes holds her gaze. So does she.

HUGHES
Is there something you want to ask me, Chief?

ELXA
(pleasantly)
No.

HUGHES
(beat, thrown)
No?

ELXA
No. I want to give you something.

She slides a folder across the desk. The label reads: "FBI LIAISON — BLACKWATER TASK FORCE."

ELXA (CONT'D)
As of tomorrow, you're my liaison to the FBI's Operation Deep Water. Special Agent Kate Doyle. You'll sit in on their briefings. You'll carry our updates to them and theirs back to me.
(beat)
Everything that passes between this department and the Bureau goes through you.

Hughes stares at the folder. Then at her.

HUGHES
(slowly)
Why me?

ELXA
Because you have twenty-five years, Warren. And contacts I'll never have. And because you told the City Council yesterday you weren't in the loop.
(beat)
Now you're the loop.

HUGHES
(beat)
After this morning.

ELXA
(evenly)
After this morning.

He looks at her for a long time. He's a good cop. He knows exactly what she's doing. And he knows exactly what she's not doing.

She isn't firing him.

HUGHES
(quietly)
You know.

ELXA
(beat)
I know that everybody in this building gets one mistake, Deputy Chief. My father-in-law taught me that. I believe he taught you.
(beat)
You've had yours.

The words sit on the desk between them.

Hughes picks up the folder. His hand is not quite steady.

HUGHES
(finally)
Yes, Chief.

ELXA
Doyle's expecting you at nine. Be early. She likes early.

He stands. He goes to the door. He stops with his hand on the knob.

HUGHES
(without turning)
Harold Jackson made me a sergeant in 1985. He said I'd be Chief someday.

ELXA
I know.

HUGHES
(beat)
He was wrong.

ELXA
(beat)
He wasn't wrong about what kind of cop you were, Warren.
(beat)
Be that one.

He goes. The door closes.

Elxa sits back. She lets out a long breath she's been holding since nine o'clock that morning.

She picks up her phone. She dials.

ELXA (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Harold. It's Elxa.
(beat)
No, sir. Nothing's wrong.
(beat)
I just wanted to tell you. It was a closed door.

She listens. Her face softens.

ELXA (CONT'D)
Yes, sir. I'll tell him.

CUT TO:

INT. DEE'S KITCHEN — WEST END — THE BACK OFFICE — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 7:14 PM EST

A cramped office behind the kitchen: invoices on a spike, a church calendar, a box of the new Hobart dishwasher's manuals, and Mama Dee's wedding photo in its silver frame on the desk.

Mama Dee sits in a swivel chair with the phone to her ear and her apron still on.

MAMA DEE
(into phone)
—same Nobody, Silas. That's what I'm telling you. Tall. White. Cap. Moves like a soldier. Your nephew ate with him Monday. Our people saw the same man drive the van in the park.

INTERCUT WITH:

^INT. MERRIWEATHER & SONS FUNERAL HOME — SILAS'S OFFICE — CONTINUOUS

Silas at his rolltop, in a cardigan, his reading glasses on and the silver fork beside his blotter.

SILAS
(slowly)
The same man.

MAMA DEE
The same man.

SILAS
(beat)
Otis found the car. Hertz. Airport. Paid cash, but they make you show a license. Otis's cousin works the counter.
(beat)
The license was from New Zealand.

MAMA DEE
New Zealand?

SILAS
That's what the girl remembered. A long foreign license with a fern on it. Name she didn't remember. She said he was very polite and he tipped her twenty dollars for a map he didn't need.

MAMA DEE
(beat)
Polite.

SILAS
Polite.

A pause on both ends.

MAMA DEE
Silas. My boy says when we find him, we share. Like we said at the funeral.

SILAS
I heard him at the funeral.
(beat)
Dolores. My nephew won't talk to me. He's talking to a stranger with a fern on his license. That stranger's teaching him something. I don't know what yet.

MAMA DEE
Then keep him close.

SILAS
I'm trying.
(beat)
Tell your boy Friday.

MAMA DEE
He'll have a name Friday.

SILAS
(beat, softening)
Did you like the lilies?

MAMA DEE
(beat, a smile she keeps out of her voice)
They came in a hearse, Silas.

SILAS
It's the only vehicle I trust.

MAMA DEE
(laughing, finally)
Goodnight, you old fool.

SILAS
(smiling)
Goodnight, Dolores.

They hang up. Mama Dee sits a moment. She looks at the wedding photograph. She touches the frame.

MAMA DEE
(to the photograph)
Don't look at me like that. He's just a friend.

CUT TO:

INT. COLONY SQUARE — PARKING GARAGE — LEVEL 4 — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 8:04 PM EST

A concrete parking deck in Midtown. Fluorescent tubes. A sign with an arrow: HOTEL ENTRANCE — LEVEL 1. Rows of cars.

A black Jaguar XJ8 turns in off the ramp and glides into a space near the elevators. The engine stops. Nobody gets out.

Thirty seconds pass.

A second car comes up the ramp. Not a Mercedes. A plain gray Ford Taurus, the kind of rental car nobody looks at twice. It parks three spaces from the Jaguar.

The Taurus door opens. A man gets out in a camel overcoat with the collar turned up, a Braves cap pulled low.

Robert Bullock.

He glances once around the deck. He walks to the Jaguar. The passenger door opens from the inside before he reaches it.

He doesn't get in. He leans down to the open door.

We can't hear what he says. We can see Camille's hand come out of the dark interior and rest against his cheek.

He closes his eyes.

Then the Jaguar's door closes. Camille gets out on the driver's side in a long black coat. They walk to the elevator. Not together. Six feet apart. Strangers who happen to be going the same way.

The elevator doors close.

On the ramp, the fluorescent light buzzes over the empty Taurus with its rental-company barcode on the back window.

CUT TO:

INT. HARMONY'S TOWNHOUSE — LINDBERGH — FRONT DOOR — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 8:13 PM EST

The doorbell rings.

Harmony, in sweatpants with her hair wrapped, looks through the peephole. She closes her eyes.

HARMONY
(through the door)
I said no more flowers.

AMOND (O.S.)
No flowers.

She opens the door. Amond stands on the step in his camel coat. He's holding a stuffed giraffe, older and more worn than the one Lyric carries everywhere. One ear's been sewn back on.

HARMONY
(beat)
That's Other Raffe.

AMOND
She asked for him on the phone last night. She said Raffe's lonely without Other Raffe. So.

He holds it out. Harmony takes it.

Behind her, from the living room, a delighted shriek.

LYRIC (O.S.)
OTHER RAFFE!

Lyric barrels down the hall in footie pajamas and seizes the second giraffe from her mother's hands. She runs back to the couch with one giraffe under each arm.

Harmony and Amond stand in the doorway. Neither moves.

AMOND
I didn't know.

HARMONY
(tired)
I know.

AMOND
(beat)
You know?

HARMONY
Esther said you probably didn't. And you sounded like you didn't. And you've never been a good liar. Two years of letters through lawyers, and you can't even lie in one of those.

AMOND
(a small, rueful smile)
My lawyer says that too.

HARMONY
Your lawyer's right.

AMOND
(beat)
My mother filed the hearing request last week. When you said "not yet." Then she changed her mind Tuesday, and it was too late.
(beat)
She's writing you a letter. An apology. For the desk.
(beat)
But she won't withdraw the petition.

HARMONY
(beat)
I know that too.

AMOND
And I'm not going to fight you in court, Harmony.

She looks up at him.

AMOND (CONT'D)
Whatever my mother does. Whatever her lawyer does. I'm not going to stand up in a courtroom and say one word against you.
(beat)
You're a good mother. I'll say that. That's all I'll say.

A long silence. Somewhere in the living room, Lyric is introducing Raffe to Other Raffe.

HARMONY
(quietly)
Why?

AMOND
(beat)
Because you are one.

Harmony looks at him. Her eyes are shining. She holds it together.

HARMONY
(finally)
Esther says let a judge say it.

AMOND
(beat)
Esther's smart.

HARMONY
Esther's terrifying.

AMOND
(a small smile)
That too.

HARMONY
(beat)
Goodnight, Amond.

AMOND
Goodnight.

She starts to close the door. Then she stops it halfway.

HARMONY
(through the gap)
The peonies are still alive.

AMOND
(beat)
Yeah?

HARMONY
Don't send more.

She closes the door. Not a slam. Gently.

Amond stands on the step for a moment. He's grinning like an idiot.

He walks back to the Range Rover.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT SIX

INT. DONOHUE MANOR — THE MASTER SUITE — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 9:36 PM EST

The grand master suite, a room Victor hasn't slept in for a week: a four-poster bed, a fire, silk walls, and photos of forty-five years of marriage on every surface.

Victor stands in the doorway in his pajamas and robe, holding his pillow under his arm like a boy at summer camp.

Joan sits up in the big bed in a silk nightgown, reading glasses on, reading a novel. She doesn't look up.

JOAN
Well, don't just stand there.

VICTOR
I'm waiting to be invited.

JOAN
(turning a page)
You're invited.

He crosses the room. He puts his pillow on his side of the bed. He sits down on the edge of the mattress and lets out a long, heartfelt breath.

VICTOR
(closing his eyes)
Lord. The Chesterfield is a torture device.

JOAN
Alexander bought it in 1952.

VICTOR
Alexander never slept on it.

JOAN
Alexander never needed to.

He laughs. He gets under the covers. Joan closes her book and takes off her reading glasses.

JOAN (CONT'D)
(quietly)
We're even.

VICTOR
(beat)
Are we?

JOAN
For the mayor. We're even.
(beat)
The next time you call a mayor about one of our grandchildren without telling me, it's fourteen nights.

VICTOR
(beat)
Understood.

She reaches over and turns off her lamp. The room goes gold with firelight.

They lie side by side in the dark for a moment.

VICTOR (CONT'D)
Joan.

JOAN
Hmm.

VICTOR
I asked Winston to pull the security tapes from the eighty-eighth floor. October fourteenth. The day "M." signed for the Harbour Point file.

JOAN
And?

VICTOR
The system tapes over itself every ninety days. October fourteenth is gone.
(beat)
Somebody knew that.

Joan turns her head on the pillow to look at him in the firelight.

JOAN
Victor.

VICTOR
I know.

JOAN
Not tonight.

VICTOR
(beat)
Not tonight.

She reaches across and takes his hand under the covers. He holds on.

VICTOR (CONT'D)
(barely)
I missed you.

JOAN
(eyes closing)
I know. You snore in the library. Winston told me. He could hear it from the kitchen.

Victor laughs softly in the dark.

The fire burns. Forty-five years of photographs watch over them.

CUT TO:

INT. THE BULLOCK PAVILION — LIVING ROOM — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 13, 2005 — 10:44 PM EST

Marc's glass house on the bluff. The living room is dark except for the fire and the lights of the city far away through the glass. Alexander's pocket watch ticks on the coffee table.

Marc sits on the couch in a T-shirt and sweatpants with a legal pad on his knee: a list in his neat handwriting. HARBOUR POINT. KRUGER. TEDDY. COLONY SQ.? He crosses nothing out.

A knock at the glass door. Not the front door. The side door, by the terrace. Two soft knocks.

Marc looks up.

Arianna stands outside the glass in a long black coat, hands in her pockets. Her breath fogs.

He crosses the room and opens the door. The cold comes in with her.

MARC
You walked.

ARIANNA
Through the garden paths. I didn't want the gatehouse list to say my car was here again.
(beat)
Your great-grandmother reads it.

MARC
(beat)
She told me.

He closes the door behind her. She doesn't take off her coat. She stands in the middle of the dark living room, in front of the fire.

ARIANNA
She interviewed me today. Carla. At two o'clock.

MARC
I know. You texted.

ARIANNA
I didn't text you what she asked.

Marc waits.

ARIANNA (CONT'D)
Four questions. The same four for all two hundred and eleven people at the Hendersons'. That's how she does it. She makes it fair so nobody can say it wasn't.
(beat)
Question one. Was I there. I said yes.
(beat)
Question two. When did I leave and which way did I drive. I said a little after two. South on Northside. West on Paces.
(beat)
She said, "Toward the curve."

Marc says nothing. His face doesn't move.

ARIANNA (CONT'D)
Question three.
(beat, her voice very small)
"Between one-thirty and three o'clock that morning, did you see anyone walking on Northside Drive?"

A long silence. The watch ticks on the table.

ARIANNA (CONT'D)
(barely)
I said no.

MARC
(quietly)
Ari.

ARIANNA
I looked her right in the eye and said no. And then I kept talking. I told her it was dark and there were no sidewalks and no streetlights and I was watching the road.
(beat)
I've cross-examined three hundred witnesses, Marc. I know what it means when somebody answers the question and then keeps talking.
(beat)
So does she. She wrote it down. I watched her write it down.

MARC
(beat)
What did she write?

ARIANNA
I couldn't see. It was short.

She finally sits, on the edge of the coffee table, facing him. Her hands are shaking in her lap.

ARIANNA (CONT'D)
And then at the door. Not a question. "Just making conversation."
(beat)
She asked what I was driving back then.

Marc goes absolutely still.

MARC
(very quietly)
What did you tell her?

ARIANNA
The truth. A Volvo.
(beat)
She said, "Fathers do." Like it was nothing.

Marc stands up. He goes to the glass wall and looks out at the dark bluff, the river below, the far-off city.

His back is to her. She can't see his face. We can.

He's thinking. Fast.

ARIANNA (CONT'D)
(to his back)
I lied to her, Marc. To a woman who put his picture on my wall.
(beat)
I told Harmony I'd find him. I told her mother I'd bring him home. And today I sat in my office under his picture and lied to the only person in three years who's actually looking.

She breaks. Silently. Her face in her hands.

MARC
(not turning, low)
You didn't lie to her about the one thing she can check.

ARIANNA
(through her hands)
What?

MARC
(turning)
You told her the truth about the car. You told her you were driving. You told her which way you went. You told her you were at the party.
(beat)
The only thing you said that wasn't true is the one thing nobody alive can contradict.

ARIANNA
(looking up, stricken)
That's what makes it a lie, Marc.

MARC
(beat)
I know.

He crosses to her. He crouches in front of the coffee table so they're eye to eye. He takes her shaking hands.

MARC (CONT'D)
(gently)
Ari. Look at me.

She looks at him.

MARC (CONT'D)
You are the best person I know. You're the only person in that courthouse who'd have reopened that file. You did it knowing what it might cost you.
(beat)
That's not a liar. That's a woman carrying something too heavy to carry alone.

ARIANNA
(barely)
Then help me carry it.

MARC
I've been carrying it with you for three years.

ARIANNA
Not like this. Not with her looking.

A long beat. The fire crackles. The watch ticks.

Something changes in Marc's face. The tenderness doesn't leave, but something colder rises up behind it, steady and clear.

Sovereign.

MARC
(very quietly)
Then tell me everything about Carla Mims.

ARIANNA
(beat)
Marc.

MARC
Where she lives. Who she's married to. What she drives. What church she goes to. Who she calls when she's stuck. How she takes her coffee.
(beat)
Everything.

ARIANNA
(pulling her hands back, frightened now)
Marc. She's a good woman. She's doing her job.

MARC
(evenly)
I know she is.

ARIANNA
You can't—

MARC
I'm not going to hurt her, Ari.
(beat)
I'm going to understand her.

Arianna stares at him. The man she's known since they were children, crouched in front of her in a T-shirt, holding her hands. And the other one, looking out through his eyes.

ARIANNA
(barely)
What's the difference?

Marc doesn't answer.

HOLD on the two of them in the firelight. On the coffee table beside them, Alexander's watch keeps ticking.

FADE OUT.

> END OF EPISODE #0009 <

===

> NEXT ON BEYOND THE KINGDOM... <

!— In the Ghost Room at 1 AM, KEISHA breaks into a stone-cutting shop's order book in Johannesburg: "Two thousand discs. Six crests. Shipped to Savannah."

!— On the Savannah waterfront, ROMAN reads a customs filing for a container from Durban. The attorney of record is BULLOCK & ASSOCIATES. MARC: "That's my father's firm."

!— In an empty Midtown parking garage at 11:48 PM, MARTIN slides a thick envelope across the hood of a car to a tall man in a cap. "Again. Bigger." The man tips his cap. "Someone they can't ignore."

> FADE TO BLACK. <
